# QEMU virt self-test of tools/nt98690/payload/probe.S
#
# Not board evidence. This is the same instruction stream the H1V1 payload
# is built from, retargeted at QEMU virt via -DSLIME_PROBE_QEMU_VIRT and run
# to prove the code executes at all before it is carried to hardware.
#
# Command:
#   qemu-system-aarch64 -machine virt,virtualization=on,gic-version=2 \
#     -cpu cortex-a72 -smp 1 -m 1G -nographic -no-reboot \
#     -kernel build/nt98690-payload/probe-qemu.bin
#
# Expected differences from a board run, both by construction:
#   check placement = FAIL   QEMU loads at its own RAM base + text_offset
#                            (0x40000000 + 0x10000000), not the link address.
#   cnt_hz_est               QEMU does not throttle its console to the line
#                            rate the estimate assumes, so the value is a
#                            fraction of the true 62.5 MHz CNTFRQ_EL0 above.
#
# The core is a Cortex-A72 here because QEMU has no Cortex-A73 model; the
# H1V1 reports midr_part 0xd09 where this reports 0xd08. That the decode is
# right is the claim, not the value.
#
# Recorded 2026-09-01T14:33:34Z on Linux 7.0.0-30-generic
# qemu: QEMU emulator version 11.0.1
# probe-qemu.bin sha256: b432c3d56006de8bc568136d085dca957273052728eefafbc6250b47d0b22d00
# board payload sha256:  1046ed432ceea7fda174a0db8086b7df1853edd89344e865c4b3ed0555c86d8e  (same source, board addresses)

